Welcome to notebook two. This is the language proper. We start at the smallest piece, which is how Scala 3 names a value, what type that value gets, and how those named values combine into the larger expressions that make up a program.

There are three mental shifts to absorb here. The first shift is that immutability is the default. The keyword val, short for value, is the everyday binding in Scala. The keyword var, short for variable, is the exception. The second shift is that the compiler infers types you do not write. You can still write them out, and sometimes you should, but inference covers most of the surface. The third shift is the largest one. In Scala, if, while, and for are not statements. They are expressions. They return values. That single fact reshapes how the rest of the language is built.

Let us start with val and var. The keyword val binds a name to a value once. After the binding, the name cannot be reassigned. If you try to reassign it, the compiler stops you. The keyword var binds a name to a value but lets you reassign it later. So val is single assignment. Var is mutable assignment.

The rule of thumb in Scala is straightforward. Reach for val first. Only switch to var when you have a clear reason, which is usually a hot loop counter, or a small piece of locally mutable state. Almost all of Spark's application programming interfaces are designed around immutable data, because immutability is what makes data safe to share across executors in a distributed system. When two threads or two machines look at the same value, immutability means they cannot disagree about what that value is.

Two things are worth registering about val. The first is that val is single assignment, not deep freeze. A val can point to a mutable collection. The binding cannot change, but the thing it points to can. We will explore that gap in detail in notebook four when we meet collections. The second thing is that the right hand side of a val is evaluated once, eagerly. The moment the line runs, the expression is computed and the name is bound to the result.

Now let us talk about types. Every binding in Scala has a static type. Most of the time you do not write that type yourself. The compiler infers it from the right hand side of the binding. If you write val n equals 42, the compiler infers the type Int. If you write val pi equals 3 point 14, the compiler infers the type Double. If you write val name equals quote ganesh quote, the compiler infers the type String.

You can also write the type explicitly. The syntax is name, colon, type, equals, value. So you could write val n colon Int equals 42, or val pi colon Double equals 3 point 14. The compiler checks that the right hand side really is of the type you claimed.

When should you write the type explicitly? Three cases come up often. The first case is a public method or a value exported from a library. Writing the type documents the contract. It pins the type against accidental change. The second case is when the inferred type is wider or narrower than what you want. Writing the type forces the compiler to use your type. The third case is when the right hand side is non trivial and a reader benefits from seeing the type at the binding site. For local bindings inside a method body, leave inference to do its job. Less noise, faster to read.

Now the core types you will use daily. Scala's primitive numeric types compile down to java virtual machine primitives, which is to say they are as fast as java integers and floats. There are four integer types, distinguished by their width. Byte is eight bits. Short is sixteen bits. Int is thirty two bits. Long is sixty four bits. There are two floating point types. Float is thirty two bits. Double is sixty four bits.

Two important defaults. An integer literal like the number forty two is inferred as Int. A floating point literal like three point one four is inferred as Double. To get a Long literal, you suffix it with a capital L. To get a Float literal, you suffix it with a lowercase f. Numeric literals also accept underscores as visual separators, so one million can be written as one underscore zero zero zero underscore zero zero zero. The compiler ignores the underscores.

Beyond the numbers, you have Boolean, which has just two values, true and false. You have Char, which is a sixteen bit unsigned integer carrying a UTF sixteen code unit. Use single quotes for a Char literal, like the letter g in single quotes. Double quotes always mean String. Speaking of String, it is not a Scala primitive. It is java's String class, reused directly. So every method you remember from java strings is available on a Scala String.

The last special type is Unit. Unit has exactly one inhabitant, written as a pair of empty parentheses. Unit is what a method returns when it returns nothing useful. It is Scala's equivalent of java's void, but unlike void it is a real type, so you can write it down, ascribe it, and pass it around.

Now a small but useful tool called type ascription. Sometimes you want to tell the compiler exactly which type to use for an expression, often to upcast to a parent type or to pin a numeric width. You write the expression followed by a colon and then the type. For example, the literal forty two written with ascription as forty two colon Long tells the compiler to treat forty two as a Long, not as the default Int.

Ascription with a colon is checked at compile time. The compiler verifies that the expression really is of that type, or that it can be widened to that type. Compare that to a different operation called as instance of, which is a runtime cast. A runtime cast bypasses the compiler's check and can throw an exception at runtime. Prefer ascription. Reach for as instance of only when interop with java reflection forces your hand.

Now we arrive at the most important idea in this notebook. In Scala, almost everything is an expression. To explain what that means, let me contrast it with the C family of languages, which includes java, C plus plus, and C sharp. In those languages, code is a sequence of statements. A statement is a thing you do, not a thing that has a value. An if is a statement. It does not return anything. A while is a statement. A block of curly braces is a statement.

In Scala, almost everything is an expression. An expression is code that evaluates to a value of some type. A block returns its last expression. An if returns whichever branch was taken. A for, when written with the yield keyword, returns a collection. Statements still exist, but they are confined to two narrow cases. Assignments to a var evaluate to Unit. While loops evaluate to Unit. Everything else gives you back a value you can bind to a name, return from a method, or pass on to another function.

Let us see what that looks like in practice. Take an if expression. Because if and else returns a value, you assign the result directly to a name. There is no need for a ternary operator like the question mark colon syntax in java, because plain if and else already returns a value. Scala 3 introduced the keyword then, which sits between the condition and the first branch. So you write, val parity equals if n modulo two equals zero then the string even else the string odd. The variable parity ends up bound to one of the two strings. Its type is inferred as String.

The brace style with parentheses around the condition still works for backward compatibility, but Scala 3's idiomatic form drops the parentheses and uses the then keyword.

A subtle but important detail. If you omit the else branch, the type of the if expression becomes the least upper bound of the then branch and Unit. In almost every case that least upper bound is the universal type Any, which is essentially useless. So in practice, any if that is supposed to produce a value must have an else.

Next is the block expression. A block, written either with curly braces or with significant indentation, is itself an expression. Its value is the value of the last expression inside it. So if you write a block that binds two local values and then computes a result, the block's value is that final computed result, and you can assign that to a name on the outside.

Two notes on blocks. First, names bound inside a block are local. They go out of scope when the block ends, either at the closing brace or at the dedent. Second, a block with no expressions has type Unit. A block that ends with an assignment also has type Unit, because assignments themselves return Unit.

Now, the holdout. While is the one structure in Scala that is purely a statement. It returns Unit. It exists for hot inner loops where mutation is the most efficient option, but in idiomatic Scala you rarely need it. The Scala 3 syntax for while uses the keyword do between the condition and the body. So you write, while i less than three do, and then on the next line, indented, you write what the loop body should do.

Why is while a statement and not an expression? Because to make while return a useful value, the loop would either need to accumulate something, which is what fold and for yield are for, or it would need to commit to mutation, which is what var already gives you. Scala draws the line. If you need a value back from a loop, use for yield. If you need imperative mutation, use while.

Speaking of for, let us preview it briefly. The for expression in Scala has two forms. The first form is for, then a generator clause like i taking values from one to three, then the keyword do, then a body. This form runs the body once per element, for its side effect, and the whole for expression evaluates to Unit. The second form is for, then a generator clause, then the keyword yield, then an expression. This form transforms each element of the source collection and gathers the results into a new collection. So if you write for i taking values from one to five yield i times i, you get back an indexed sequence containing the numbers one, four, nine, sixteen, twenty five.

The yield form is the one you will reach for constantly in idiomatic Scala. Pull elements out of a collection, transform each one, get a new collection back. Internally, for yield is syntactic sugar over the methods map, flatMap, and withFilter. We will desugar it step by step in notebook five, when we cover collection operations.

One more flavour of binding worth knowing now. The lazy val. A lazy val postpones evaluation of its right hand side until the first time the name is actually read. After that first read, the computed value is cached, so subsequent reads are instant. Lazy val is how you defer expensive work until it is genuinely needed. It is also how you break otherwise circular initialisation between two values that depend on each other.

Use lazy val sparingly though. Laziness can hide where work actually happens in your program. When a bug appears, you may have to chase the laziness chain back to find out which read triggered the original computation. Useful, but a debugging cost when overused.

Let us close with a small composition that uses everything from this notebook. Take a score, say seventy three. Compute a grade, where the grade is built inside an outer block. Inside that outer block, you bind a local val called letter, which is itself the value of an if, else if, else if, else expression that chooses one of A, B, C, or F based on the score. After binding letter, the outer block's final expression is an interpolated string that combines the score and the letter into a sentence. That interpolated string is what the outer val, called grade, binds to.

Notice three things about that composition. First, the outer val grade equals binds the value of the block, which is the final interpolated string. Second, the inner val letter equals if and else binds the value of the if chain. Third, nothing was reassigned. No var. No early returns. The shape is, name a value, derive the next value from it, return the last one. That is the rhythm of idiomatic Scala.

Now that you can name values and combine them into expressions, notebook three moves up one level. Functions and methods. There you will see how Scala turns the lambda calculus idea of functions as values into something you can actually pass to map, return from another function, and store in a collection. That building block is what every later notebook in this track depends on.
